d('B003','retain first; contextual and certainty differences','The main horizontal line supplies the opening parenthesis, voire with a low final e and comma, la notion de, paired quotes around lieu ponctuel and point, then dont il n’a pas encore été question, three dots and closing parenthesis. The crossing diagonal strokes belong to the marginal note and do not remove dont il n’a. The extra de lieu and Une des raisons en est in the blind excerpt are neighbouring context already present in the page. Three literal dots and ldots represent the same observed ellipsis; retain the page encoding.')
d('B004','retain main reading; extend cancelled-word doubt','The rotated horizontal baselines of the marginal note show L’autre raison, c’est que, dans le cas d’une; the long quoted word has the pp descenders, l ascender and -cation sequence of application, followed by a separate f, rather than localité/localisation. The next quoted group has the r lead-in, descending g and ending -ion of région; it continues dans une autre, not dans un site. The quote ticks around figure and the parenthesis remain visible among the crossing body strokes. Inside it, peut donner un sens is readable: the looped d and connected -onner exclude être, while géométrique, à, ce and mot/terme remain explicitly doubtful. After the cancellation, the upright crossed t and en-d strokes support tend; the following à ne, doubtful pas and encore, and être precede une figure. This is not a clearly written tandis que. The lower line shows mais l’image réciproque d’une, with the p descender and intervening c-r-o group; it is not a second inverse. The two multistr. forms stop after str and have abbreviation points, rather than fully written multistrate. The short est and une are retained, rather than sera encore un. No line-break command is source ink. Punctuation and existing doubts are retained; the cancelled-word alternatives are enlarged as separately checked in B005.',['003-A01'])
d('B005','mark doubt','Multiple cancellation strokes obscure the initial letters and central joining strokes. The low final group favours sera but does not let every letter exclude devient/devant. Keep the deletion and add the blind alternatives; do not resolve cancelled ink from syntax.',['003-A01'])
d('B006','retain page; scope-only difference','The main baseline shows où followed by the dotted i and looped l of il; the latter is already present immediately after où in the page. The diagonal strokes above are the margin note.',final='où il')
d('B007','retain first','The main word ends with a distinct crossed x and starts with a looped l, dotted i and flattened eu. The descending diagonal passing the word belongs to the marginal note, not to a g. Retain lieux rather than figures.')
d('B008','retain first','The first group shows e with its accent and a tall t; the separated low an run ends at a tall terminal t. It reads étant. The following des is on the next main baseline and is not this boxed word.')
d('B010','retain first','After l’ the compact e-n-s run ends in a flat rightward s tail before de, not a separately closed final e of une. The parenthesis and the strates quote ticks are visible; the long insertion leader descends before comme. Retain par l’ens and the original attachment.')
d('B011','retain first','The l-apostrophe is followed by the same low ens sequence, including the long terminal s tail, before the M. The letter M is clear, but the crop does not establish an intended typographical alphabet. Retain the first-definition M/calligraphic-M doubt and the abbreviation ens.')
d('B014','retain page; scope-only difference','The comma is joined to the M’s right exit and is already present after M in the complete page. No additional punctuation is required.',final='$M$,')
d('B021','retain first','The strike covers the short word’s diagnostic opening and joining strokes. Neither le nor il is certain; preserve both alternatives in their existing order and retain the deletion.')
d('B022','retain first','The word has the s-u opening, paired p descenders and final rt. Raised closing quotation strokes lie at the upper-right edge; the narrow excerpt leaves the initial quotation region partly beyond its edge. The blind plain word does not establish absence of the quotation marks. Retain the quoted support already read in the complete source context.')
